e view having the same data-type\n3127 |             as `a`. As with ``ndarray.view``, dtype can also be specified as\n3128 |             an ndarray sub-class, which then specifies the type of the\n3129 |             returned object (this is equivalent to setting the ``type``\n3130 |             parameter).\n3131 |         type : Python type, optional\n3132 |             Type of the returned view, either ndarray or a subclass.  The\n3133 |             default None results in type preservation.\n3134 |         fill_value : scalar, optional\n3135 |             The value to use for invalid entries (None by default).\n3136 |             If None, then this argument is inferred from the passed `dtype`, or\n3137 |             in its absence the original array, as discussed in the notes below.\n3138 | \n3139 |         See Also\n3140 |         --------\n3141 |         numpy.ndarray.view : Equivalent method on ndarray object.\n3142 | \n3143 |         Notes\n3144 |         -----\n3145 | \n3146 |         ``a.view()`` is used two different ways:\n3147 | \n3148 |         ``a.view(some_dtype)`` or ``a.view(dtype=some_dtype)`` constructs a view\n3149 |         of the array's memory with a different data-type.  This can cause a\n3150 |         reinterpretation of the bytes of memory.\n3151 | \n3152 |         ``a.view(ndarray_subclass)`` or ``a.view(type=ndarray_subclass)`` just\n3153 |         returns an instance of `ndarray_subclass` that looks at the same array\n3154 |         (same shape, dtype, etc.)  This does not cause a reinterpretation of the\n3155 |         memory.\n3156 | \n3157 |         If `fill_value` is not specified, but `dtype` is specified (and is not\n3158 |         an ndarray sub-class), the `fill_value` of the MaskedArray will be\n3159 |         reset. If neither `fill_value` nor `dtype` are specified (or if\n3160 |         `dtype` is an ndarray sub-class), then the fill value is preserved.\n3161 |         Finally, if `fill_value` is specified, but `dtype` is not, the fill\n3162 |         value is set to the specified value.\n3163 | \n3164 |         For ``a.view(some_dtype)``, if ``some_dtype`` has a different number of\n3165 |         bytes per entry than the previous dtype (for example, converting a\n3166 |         regular array to a structured array), then the behavior of the view\n3167 |         cannot be predicted just from the superficial appearance of ``a`` (shown\n3168 |         by ``print(a)``). It also depends on exactly how ``a`` is stored in\n3169 |         memory. Therefore if ``a`` is C-ordered versus fortran-ordered, versus\n3170 |         defined as a slice or transpose, etc., the view may give different\n3171 |         results.\n3172 |         \"\"\"\n3173 | \n3174 |         if dtype is None:\n3175 |             if type is None:\n3176 |                 output = ndarray.view(self)\n3177 |             else:\n3178 |                 output = ndarray.view(self, type)\n3179 |         elif type is None:\n3180 |             try:\n3181 |                 if issubclass(dtype, ndarray):\n3182 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n3183 |                     dtype = None\n3184 |                 else:\n3185 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n3186 |             except TypeError:\n3187 |                 output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n3188 |         else:\n3189 |             output = ndarray.view(self, dtype, type)\n3190 | \n3191 |         # also make the mask be a view (so attr changes to the view's\n3192 |         # mask do no affect original object's mask)\n3193 |         # (especially important to avoid affecting np.masked singleton)\n3194 |         if getmask(output) is not nomask:\n3195 |             output._mask = output._mask.view()\n3196 | \n3197 |         # Make sure to reset the _fill_value if needed\n3198 |         if getattr(output, '_fill_value', None) is not None:\n3199 |             if fill_value is None:\n3200 |                 if dtype is None:\n3201 |                     pass  # leave _fill_value as is\n3202 |                 else:\n3203 |                     output._fill_value = None\n3204 |             else:\n3205 |                 output.fill_value = fill_value\n3206 |         return output\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/ma/mrecords.py:MaskedRecords.view`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `view`.\n```\n361 | def view(self, dtype=None, type=None):\n362 |         \"\"\"\n363 |         Returns a view of the mrecarray.\n364 | \n365 |         \"\"\"\n366 |         # OK, basic copy-paste from MaskedArray.view.\n367 |         if dtype is None:\n368 |             if type is None:\n369 |                 output = ndarray.view(self)\n370 |             else:\n371 |                 output = ndarray.view(self, type)\n372 |         # Here again.\n373 |         elif type is None:\n374 |             try:\n375 |                 if issubclass(dtype, ndarray):\n376 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n377 |                     dtype = None\n378 |                 else:\n379 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n380 |             # OK, there's the change\n381 |             except TypeError:\n382 |                 dtype = np.dtype(dtype)\n383 |                 # we need to revert to MaskedArray, but keeping the possibility\n384 |                 # of subclasses (eg, TimeSeriesRecords), so we'll force a type\n385 |                 # set to the first parent\n386 |                 if dtype.fields is None:\n387 |                     basetype = self.__class__.__bases__[0]\n388 |                     output = self.__array__().view(dtype, basetype)\n389 |                     output._update_from(self)\n390 |                 else:\n391 |                     output = ndarray.view(self, dtype)\n392 |                 output._fill_value = None\n393 |         else:\n394 |             output = ndarray.view(self, dtype, type)\n395 |         # Update the mask, just like in MaskedArray.view\n396 |         if (getattr(output, '_mask', nomask) is not nomask):\n397 |             mdtype = ma.make_mask_descr(output.dtype)\n398 |             output._mask = self._mask.view(mdtype, ndarray)\n399 |             output._mask.shape = output.shape\n400 |         return output\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"window\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase.window`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `window`.\n```\n109 | def window(self):\n110 |         pass\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"strided\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/core/_internal.py:_ctypes.strides`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n334 | def strides(self):\n335 |         \"\"\"\n336 |         (c_intp*self.ndim): A ctypes array of length self.ndim where\n337 |         the basetype is the same as for the shape attribute. This ctypes array\n338 |         contains the strides information from the underlying array. This strides\n339 |         information is important for showing how many bytes must be jumped to\n340 |         get to the next element in the array.\n341 |         \"\"\"\n342 |         return self.strides_as(_getintp_ctype())\n```\n\nFound class `benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_UFunc`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n25 | class AVX_UFunc(Benchmark):\n26 |     params = [avx_ufuncs, stride, dtype]\n27 |     param_names = ['avx_based_ufunc', 'stride', 'dtype']\n28 |     timeout = 10\n29 | \n30 |     def setup(self, ufuncname, stride, dtype):\n31 |         np.seterr(all='ignore')\n32 |         try:\n33 |             self.f = getattr(np, ufuncname)\n34 |         except AttributeError:\n35 |             raise NotImplementedError()\n36 |         N = 10000\n37 |         self.arr = np.ones(stride*N, dtype)\n38 | \n39 |     def time_ufunc(self, ufuncname, stride, dtype):\n40 |         self.f(self.arr[::stride])\n```\n\nFound function `benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_UFunc_log.setup`.\n