onal to the values that they\n1127 |         represent. A bar plot shows comparisons among discrete categories. One\n1128 |         axis of the plot shows the specific categories being compared, and the\n1129 |         other axis represents a measured value.\n1130 |         \"\"\"\n1131 |         return self(kind=\"bar\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound file `pandas/core/exchange/dataframe.py`.\nFound file `pandas/plotting/__init__.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"DataFrame.plot\", \"subplots\", \"DataFrame.plot subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.pie`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1531 | def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n132 | def create_subplots(\n133 |     naxes: int,\n134 |     sharex: bool = False,\n135 |     sharey: bool = False,\n136 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n137 |     subplot_kw=None,\n138 |     ax=None,\n139 |     layout=None,\n140 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n141 |     **fig_kw,\n142 | ):\n143 |     \"\"\"\n144 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n145 | \n146 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n147 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n148 | \n149 |     Parameters\n150 |     ----------\n151 |     naxes : int\n152 