-> Self:\n 943 |         \"\"\"\n 944 |         Localize tz-naive Datetime Array/Index to tz-aware Datetime Array/Index.\n 945 | \n 946 |         This method takes a time zone (tz) naive Datetime Array/Index object\n 947 |         and makes this time zone aware. It does not move the time to another\n 948 |         time zone.\n 949 | \n 950 |         This method can also be used to do the inverse -- to create a time\n 951 |         zone unaware object from an aware object. To that end, pass `tz=None`.\n 952 | \n 953 |         Parameters\n 954 |         ----------\n 955 |         tz : str, zoneinfo.ZoneInfo,, pytz.timezone, dateutil.tz.tzfile, datetime.tzinfo or None\n 956 |             Time zone to convert timestamps to. Passing ``None`` will\n 957 |             remove the time zone information preserving local time.\n 958 |         ambiguous : 'infer', 'NaT', bool array, default 'raise'\n 959 |             When clocks moved backward due to DST, ambiguous times may arise.\n 960 |             For example in Central European Time (UTC+01), when going from\n 961 |             03:00 DST to 02:00 non-DST, 02:30:00 local time occurs both at\n 962 |             00:30:00 UTC and at 01:30:00 UTC. In such a situation, the\n 963 |             `ambiguous` parameter dictates how ambiguous times should be\n 964 |             handled.\n 965 | \n 966 |             - 'infer' will attempt to infer fall dst-transition hours based on\n 967 |               order\n 968 |             - bool-ndarray where True signifies a DST time, False signifies a\n 969 |               non-DST time (note that this flag is only applicable for\n 970 |               ambiguous times)\n 971 |             - 'NaT' will return NaT where there are ambiguous times\n 972 |             - 'raise' will raise a ValueError if there are ambiguous\n 973 |               times.\n 974 | \n 975 |         nonexistent : 'shift_forward', 'shift_backward, 'NaT', timedelta, \\\n 976 | default 'raise'\n 977 |             A nonexistent time does not exist in a particular timezone\n 978 |             where clocks moved forward due to DST.\n 979 | \n 980 |             - 'shift_forward' will shift the nonexistent time forward to the\n 981 |               closest existing time\n 982 |             - 'shift_backward' will shift the nonexistent time backward to the\n 983 |               closest existing time\n 984 |             - 'NaT' will return NaT where there are nonexistent times\n 985 |             - timedelta objects will shift nonexistent times by the timedelta\n 986 |             - 'raise' will raise a ValueError if there are\n 987 |               nonexistent times.\n 988 | \n 989 |         Returns\n 990 |         -------\n 991 |         Same type as self\n 992 |             Array/Index converted to the specified time zone.\n 993 | \n 994 |         Raises\n 995 |         ------\n 996 |         TypeError\n 997 |             If the Datetime Array/Index is tz-aware and tz is not None.\n 998 | \n 999 |         See Also\n1000 |         --------\n1001 |         DatetimeIndex.tz_convert : Convert tz-aware DatetimeIndex from\n1002 |             one time zone to another.\n1003 | \n1004 |         Examples\n1005 |         --------\n1006 |         >>> tz_naive = pd.date_range('2018-03-01 09:00', periods=3)\n1007 |         >>> tz_naive\n1008 |         DatetimeIndex(['2018-03-01 09:00:00', '2018-03-02 09:00:00',\n1009 |                        '2018-03-03 09:00:00'],\n1010 |                       dtype='datetime64[ns]', freq='D')\n1011 | \n1012 |         Localize DatetimeIndex in US/Eastern time zone:\n1013 | \n1014 |         >>> tz_aware = tz_naive.tz_localize(tz='US/Eastern')\n1015 |         >>> tz_aware\n1016 |         DatetimeIndex(['2018-03-01 09:00:00-05:00',\n1017 |                        '2018-03-02 09:00:00-05:00',\n1018 |                        '2018-03-03 09:00:00-05:00'],\n1019 |                       dtype='datetime64[ns, US/Eastern]', freq=None)\n1020 | \n1021 |         With the ``tz=None``, we can remove the time zone information\n1022 |         while keeping the local time (not converted to UTC):\n1023 | \n1024 |         >>> tz_aware.tz_localize(None)\n1025 |         DatetimeIndex(['2018-03-01 09:00:00', '2018-03-02 09:00:00',\n1026 |                        '2018-03-03 09:00:00'],\n1027 |                       dtype='datetime64[ns]', freq=None)\n1028 | \n1029 |         Be careful with DST changes. When there is sequential data, pandas can\n1030 |         infer the DST time:\n1031 | \n1032 |         >>> s = pd.to_datetime(pd.Series(['2018-10-28 01:30:00',\n1033 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 02:00:00',\n1034 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 02:30:00',\n1035 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 02:00:00',\n1036 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 02:30:00',\n1037 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 03:00:00',\n1038 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 03:30:00']))\n1039 |         >>> s.dt.tz_localize('CET', ambiguous='infer')\n1040 |         0   2018-10-28 01:30:00+02:00\n1041 |         1   2018-10-28 02:00:00+02:00\n1042 |         2   2018-10-28 02:30:00+02:00\n1043 |         3   2018-10-28 02:00:00+01:00\n1044 |         4   2018-10-28 02:30:00+01:00\n1045 |         5   2018-10-28 03:00:00+01:00\n1046 |         6   2018-10-28 03:30:00+01:00\n1047 |         dtype: datetime64[s, CET]\n1048 | \n1049 |         In some cases, inferring the DST is impossible. In such cases, you can\n1050 |         pass an ndarray to the ambiguous parameter to set the DST explicitly\n1051 | \n1052 |         >>> s = pd.to_datetime(pd.Series(['2018-10-28 01:20:00',\n1053 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 02:36:00',\n1054 |         ...                               '2018-10-28 03:46:00']))\n1055 |         >>> s.dt.tz_localize('CET', ambiguous=np.array([True, True, False]))\n1056 |         0   2018-10-28 01:20:00+02:00\n1057 |         