ample executed lines:\n    - `line 1012`: `return [self.index, self.columns]`\n\n2. Unique dependency tree (dataflows associated with unique artifacts):\n# Unique Dependency Hints\n\nUse these dependency hints as supplemental evidence, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace started at: `2026-03-30 15:14:45`\n- Retained files: `4`\n- Retained symbols: `28`\n- Retained edges: `36`\n- Matched dependency paths: `42`\n- Named locations are limited to the target package: `pandas`\n- External dependencies are summarized generically and are not listed by file or function name.\n\n## Related File Clusters\n- `pandas/core/generic.py`: appears in `7` retained dependency path(s)\n- `pandas/core/indexes/range.py`: appears in `1` retained dependency path(s)\n- `pandas/core/indexes/base.py`: appears in `1` retained dependency path(s)\n\n## Key Dependency Paths\n- path: `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.axes -> pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n- path: `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:__len__ -> pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n- path: `pandas/core/indexes/range.py:__len__ -> pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n- path: `pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n- path: `pandas/core/generic.py:squeeze`\n- path: `pandas/core/generic.py:squeeze -> [outside target package omitted]`\n\n## Anchor Artifacts\n- anchor: `pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n  - module: `pandas.core.generic`\n  - related files:\n    - `pandas/core/generic.py`: `5` retained path(s)\n    - `pandas/core/indexes/range.py`: `1` retained path(s)\n    - `pandas/core/indexes/base.py`: `1` retained path(s)\n  - key dependency paths:\n    - `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.axes -> pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n    - `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:__len__ -> pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n    - `pandas/core/indexes/range.py:__len__ -> pandas/core/generic.py:<genexpr>`\n- anchor: `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`\n  - module: `pandas.core.generic`\n  - definition: `lines 841-953`\n  - related files:\n    - `pandas/core/generic.py`: `2` retained path(s)\n  - key dependency paths:\n    - `pandas/core/generic.py:squeeze`\n    - `pandas/core/generic.py:squeeze -> [outside target package omitted]`"}, {"content": "I'll help analyze this documentation issue systematically following the given steps.\n\n## Step 1: Let me categorize and extract key information\n\nRequest feature description:\n- Main request: Add type hint for squeeze method\n- Location reference: pandas/core/generic.py\n\nBehavior with feature:\n- No functional changes needed\n- Documentation improvement only\n- Method: squeeze()\n\nExpected behavior:\n- None specified\n\nAdditional context:\n- Related to NDFrame.squeeze method\n- Part of pandas-dev repository\n- Documenting project\n\nLet me explore the repository structure first to locate the relevant files.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['squeeze', 'NDFrame.squeeze']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"squeeze\", \"NDFrame.squeeze\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `squeeze`.\n```\n841 | def squeeze(self, axis: Axis | None = None):\n842 |         \"\"\"\n843 |         Squeeze 1 dimensional axis objects into scalars.\n844 | \n845 |         Series or DataFrames with a single element are squeezed to a scalar.\n846 |         DataFrames with a single column or a single row are squeezed to a\n847 |         Series. Otherwise the object is unchanged.\n848 | \n849 |         This method is most useful when you don't know if your\n850 |         object is a Series or DataFrame, but you do know it has just a single\n851 |         column. In that case you can safely call `squeeze` to ensure you have a\n852 |         Series.\n853 | \n854 |         Parameters\n855 |         ----------\n856 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}, default None\n857 |             A specific axis to squeeze. By default, all length-1 axes are\n858 |             squeezed. For `Series` this parameter is unused and defaults to `None`.\n859 | \n860 |         Returns\n861 |         -------\n862 |         DataFrame, Series, or scalar\n863 |             The projection after squeezing `axis` or all the axes.\n864 | \n865 |         See Also\n866 |         --------\n867 |         Series.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting scalars.\n868 |         DataFrame.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting Series.\n869 |         Series.to_frame : Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 | 